1
00:00:01,869 --> 00:00:11,614
[SPEAKER_01]: I was wondering if you could say something about this sutta, if you had anything to say about it.

2
00:00:12,815 --> 00:00:19,519
[SPEAKER_01]: It's about Paticcasamuppāda, just a short verse, Samyutta Nikaya 12.41.

3
00:00:19,799 --> 00:00:25,562
[SPEAKER_01]: It says, and which is the noble method

4
00:00:28,694 --> 00:00:30,035
[SPEAKER_01]: When there is this, there is this.

5
00:00:30,055 --> 00:00:32,656
[SPEAKER_01]: When there isn't this, there isn't this.

6
00:00:32,696 --> 00:00:34,156
[SPEAKER_01]: When this arises, this arises.

7
00:00:34,176 --> 00:00:37,097
[SPEAKER_01]: When this ceases, this ceases.

8
00:00:56,194 --> 00:00:57,395
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's that noble method.

9
00:00:58,395 --> 00:01:01,757
[SPEAKER_00]: Noble method is noble understanding, noble discernment.

10
00:01:03,618 --> 00:01:06,699
[SPEAKER_00]: So, as the Buddha said, what method is there?

11
00:01:06,759 --> 00:01:08,820
[SPEAKER_00]: It's the one where he properly attends.

12
00:01:09,901 --> 00:01:12,922
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you can't do paticca-sampada.

13
00:01:13,862 --> 00:01:15,823
[SPEAKER_00]: You can either see it or not see it.

14
00:01:16,784 --> 00:01:20,326
[SPEAKER_00]: And usually, paticca-sampada is translated as dependent origination.

15
00:01:21,975 --> 00:01:23,576
[SPEAKER_00]: I suppose, as a translation, it's fine.

16
00:01:24,296 --> 00:01:31,179
[SPEAKER_00]: But the problem is, it carries on a view of an explanatory nature.

17
00:01:32,460 --> 00:01:34,281
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's like cause and effect.

18
00:01:34,581 --> 00:01:36,342
[SPEAKER_00]: That's how people regard paticampāda.

19
00:01:36,822 --> 00:01:40,184
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, this is the cause, this is the result, this is the new cause, this is the new result.

20
00:01:40,724 --> 00:01:43,145
[SPEAKER_00]: But it isn't, the way the Buddha described it.

21
00:01:43,845 --> 00:01:48,888
[SPEAKER_00]: It's a principle whereby, while this is present, this is also the result of it.

22
00:01:49,828 --> 00:01:51,229
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's a simultaneous presence.

23
00:01:52,259 --> 00:01:57,404
[SPEAKER_00]: So, with the presence of feeling, there is presence of craving in regard to that present feeling.

24
00:01:58,985 --> 00:02:02,128
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not your craving is the result of your feeling.

25
00:02:03,329 --> 00:02:05,631
[SPEAKER_00]: They're simultaneously present to the same extent.

26
00:02:06,552 --> 00:02:14,759
[SPEAKER_00]: Not knowing how to attend that, not seeing a necessary condition that presently endures within your enduring craving, that's how you crave.

27
00:02:17,203 --> 00:02:23,087
[SPEAKER_00]: So, Pārthitam Upāda, it's not an explanation of what happens to you.

28
00:02:23,707 --> 00:02:28,130
[SPEAKER_00]: It's a direct insight of how to uproot things that happen to you.

29
00:02:28,830 --> 00:02:31,832
[SPEAKER_00]: Liability to craving, greed, aversion, delusion, all these things.

30
00:02:31,872 --> 00:02:33,393
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why it's a noble method.

31
00:02:34,354 --> 00:02:35,995
[SPEAKER_00]: Method of overcoming suffering.

32
00:02:37,866 --> 00:02:39,026
[SPEAKER_00]: But, that's what we said.

33
00:02:39,406 --> 00:02:40,006
[SPEAKER_00]: How do you do it?

34
00:02:40,126 --> 00:02:44,507
[SPEAKER_00]: You... Noble disciple means you need to have a right view, and you do it by properly attending.

35
00:02:45,087 --> 00:02:46,808
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you're caught up in craving.

36
00:02:47,248 --> 00:02:49,668
[SPEAKER_00]: You catch yourself full of desire, for whatever.

37
00:02:50,809 --> 00:02:53,649
[SPEAKER_00]: Sense experience or desire to get rid of something.

38
00:02:55,089 --> 00:02:57,410
[SPEAKER_00]: What is the necessary basis for that desire?

39
00:02:58,610 --> 00:03:01,091
[SPEAKER_00]: Can you have desire without having craving?

40
00:03:02,171 --> 00:03:06,672
[SPEAKER_00]: Can you have... Oh, I desire such and such things, without craving those things.

41
00:03:06,732 --> 00:03:07,352
[SPEAKER_00]: Can you have that?

42
00:03:08,356 --> 00:03:08,936
[SPEAKER_00]: It's impossible.

43
00:03:11,158 --> 00:03:21,924
[SPEAKER_00]: So then, any form of desire, doesn't matter the specifics of your desire, requires presently enduring craving, attitude of craving.

44
00:03:21,944 --> 00:03:22,845
[SPEAKER_00]: And you can stop there.

45
00:03:22,925 --> 00:03:25,426
[SPEAKER_00]: Now you can say, okay, so my desire is rooted in my craving.

46
00:03:25,506 --> 00:03:27,827
[SPEAKER_00]: You're not, you know, you're not basically overcoming it.

47
00:03:28,888 --> 00:03:33,411
[SPEAKER_00]: You might have acknowledged it, which is a good first step, but that's... that won't overcome it.

48
00:03:34,184 --> 00:03:35,765
[SPEAKER_00]: So, then the Buddha said you attend further.

49
00:03:36,305 --> 00:03:39,427
[SPEAKER_00]: What is another necessary basis within craving?

50
00:03:41,208 --> 00:03:42,749
[SPEAKER_00]: Necessary basis for craving?

51
00:03:43,789 --> 00:03:44,810
[SPEAKER_00]: Feeling.

52
00:03:44,830 --> 00:03:45,230
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

53
00:03:45,510 --> 00:03:51,973
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you must be feeling either pleasant, must be feeling either unpleasant, or must be feeling either neither pleasant or unpleasant.

54
00:03:53,294 --> 00:03:59,397
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you started with, I want this so much that, you know, I'm about to do unwholesome things just to get it.

55
00:04:00,238 --> 00:04:00,878
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't do it.

56
00:04:00,918 --> 00:04:02,159
[SPEAKER_00]: You practice sense restraint.

57
00:04:03,540 --> 00:04:04,801
[SPEAKER_00]: But the desire is still there.

58
00:04:05,561 --> 00:04:11,626
[SPEAKER_00]: Through that desire, you discern the necessity of presently enduring craving, which is basically the container of that desire.

59
00:04:13,287 --> 00:04:20,532
[SPEAKER_00]: Now, through that presently enduring craving, you discern a necessary basis of presently enduring feeling, which is a container for your craving.

60
00:04:22,293 --> 00:04:26,216
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, you start dwelling, attending properly to the nature of that present feeling.

61
00:04:26,596 --> 00:04:27,377
[SPEAKER_00]: Have you created it?

62
00:04:28,938 --> 00:04:29,078
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

63
00:04:29,719 --> 00:04:30,399
[SPEAKER_00]: Did you have said it?

64
00:04:30,459 --> 00:04:30,579
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

65
00:04:30,659 --> 00:04:31,520
[SPEAKER_00]: You're subjected to it.

66
00:04:32,599 --> 00:04:35,220
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's not yours, even if you want to make it yours.

67
00:04:36,321 --> 00:04:40,623
[SPEAKER_00]: You're only basically maintaining it as yours, through not seeing it, that it isn't yours.

68
00:04:41,363 --> 00:04:47,146
[SPEAKER_00]: And through giving in, habitually, towards the desire that's already contained within the attitude of craving.

69
00:04:49,207 --> 00:04:54,609
[SPEAKER_00]: So, through giving in to the desire, you maintain ignoring the nature of the feeling, which is that it can't be yours.

70
00:04:55,089 --> 00:04:58,431
[SPEAKER_00]: Because if you stop ignoring it, you see that it can't be yours, craving disappears.

71
00:05:00,311 --> 00:05:06,597
[SPEAKER_00]: And disappears by being included within the arisen feeling, not by you choosing to, like, pick it and remove it.

72
00:05:07,017 --> 00:05:15,144
[SPEAKER_01]: So, then, when the craving disappears... Are you talking about the right... that's the right view, then?

73
00:05:15,184 --> 00:05:16,726
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, all of this, like...

74
00:05:17,888 --> 00:05:22,892
[SPEAKER_00]: If somebody has the right view already, all of this would be just further development of their right view.

75
00:05:23,873 --> 00:05:31,780
[SPEAKER_00]: But even if you don't have the right view, training yourself how to discern craving within the desire, how to discern feeling within craving.

76
00:05:32,481 --> 00:05:33,822
[SPEAKER_00]: Not before or after.

77
00:05:33,862 --> 00:05:37,325
[SPEAKER_00]: Not as a result, a psychologization or explanation.

78
00:05:37,685 --> 00:05:39,206
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, I crave because I've... No.

79
00:05:39,846 --> 00:05:42,929
[SPEAKER_00]: While you crave, you discern present enduring feeling.

80
00:05:42,969 --> 00:05:44,290
[SPEAKER_00]: So, while you crave, you feel.

81
00:05:44,970 --> 00:05:46,451
[SPEAKER_00]: And it's the craving in regard to feeling.

82
00:05:46,992 --> 00:05:51,054
[SPEAKER_00]: You cannot have craving, second longer, if the feeling disappears.

83
00:05:51,455 --> 00:05:52,355
[SPEAKER_00]: But you can have feeling.

84
00:05:53,276 --> 00:05:55,257
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, you can have feeling without craving.

85
00:05:55,558 --> 00:05:58,360
[SPEAKER_00]: But the only way to understand that is through abandoning the craving.

86
00:05:58,988 --> 00:06:02,251
[SPEAKER_00]: Not like finding out how to not crave on top of your craving.

87
00:06:03,032 --> 00:06:14,143
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the only way to abandon, to understand how you can have feeling without craving, is to first, through craving, you discern a necessary basis of feeling, and then abandon craving by fully understanding that necessary basis.

88
00:06:14,723 --> 00:06:16,405
[SPEAKER_00]: That's exactly paticcaṁ pāḍhā, nothing else.

89
00:06:16,865 --> 00:06:23,892
[SPEAKER_00]: With this as a necessary basis, this is enduring, engaging me, making me act, making me feel, all of these things.

90
00:06:25,797 --> 00:06:30,281
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why there's so many different variations of paticca upādāna, the suttas.

91
00:06:30,461 --> 00:06:33,343
[SPEAKER_00]: Sixteen factors, twelve factors, eight factors sometimes.

92
00:06:33,643 --> 00:06:35,545
[SPEAKER_00]: Some omitted, some included, some added.

93
00:06:36,586 --> 00:06:38,327
[SPEAKER_00]: Because they're the principle.

94
00:06:38,728 --> 00:06:44,092
[SPEAKER_00]: Principle of discerning the necessary basis for another simultaneously enduring phenomenon.

95
00:06:44,913 --> 00:06:45,954
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not an explanation.

96
00:06:46,474 --> 00:06:52,979
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not a... it's not a... it's not like a linear sort of set of visualizations you have to go through in order to practice it.

97
00:06:53,219 --> 00:06:53,660
[SPEAKER_00]: None of that.

98
00:06:54,830 --> 00:06:55,351
[SPEAKER_00]: It's discerning.

99
00:06:55,411 --> 00:06:56,491
[SPEAKER_00]: What is my present situation?

100
00:06:56,972 --> 00:06:58,133
[SPEAKER_00]: Is there a sense-desire in me?

101
00:06:58,893 --> 00:06:59,033
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

102
00:06:59,694 --> 00:07:01,115
[SPEAKER_00]: Is there absence of sense-desire in me?

103
00:07:01,475 --> 00:07:01,675
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes.

104
00:07:02,336 --> 00:07:05,618
[SPEAKER_00]: What is the present feeling on account of that?

105
00:07:05,679 --> 00:07:07,220
[SPEAKER_00]: Is there any desire towards that feeling?

106
00:07:07,760 --> 00:07:10,162
[SPEAKER_00]: So now I have a peaceful feeling, neutral feeling.

107
00:07:10,662 --> 00:07:13,805
[SPEAKER_00]: Do I have desire to sort of want that feeling more?

108
00:07:15,246 --> 00:07:17,888
[SPEAKER_00]: In which case, well, it's not anymore neutral, it's unpleasant now.

109
00:07:21,460 --> 00:07:37,268
[SPEAKER_00]: In regard to the body, seeing the body and external objects... Can you even fathom, can you even think, imagine, in utter, complete abstraction, an external object without the necessary basis of your body?

110
00:07:39,009 --> 00:07:39,589
[SPEAKER_01]: Impossible.

111
00:07:41,731 --> 00:07:50,195
[SPEAKER_00]: So, seeing those two... Well, seeing basically the right order of things, seeing this within this, not this within that.

112
00:07:51,612 --> 00:07:59,442
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's the difference in the, in paticabhara, like, when not seen, then, yeah, your feelings are a result of your craving, because that's how you assume them.

113
00:08:00,023 --> 00:08:07,593
[SPEAKER_00]: They're not, but you don't know that they're not, and because of that, the only reality you know is the reality that, yeah, you are craving and all this.

114
00:08:07,633 --> 00:08:08,995
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why it's like a vicious circle.

115
00:08:09,827 --> 00:08:18,991
[SPEAKER_00]: Until you understand that your craving cannot make a difference to the nature of being subjected to the feeling, and that your craving can never step outside of the feeling.

116
00:08:19,011 --> 00:08:23,872
[SPEAKER_00]: It requires feeling as a necessary container for it to endure, for it to manifest.

117
00:08:29,455 --> 00:08:38,258
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you don't want to be doing, reflecting upon what this is all about, like, on random things, because it's already subtle enough to be understood.

118
00:08:41,255 --> 00:08:42,917
[SPEAKER_00]: You're going to just make it more abstract.

119
00:08:42,937 --> 00:08:46,681
[SPEAKER_00]: You want to find practical side of it.

120
00:08:46,781 --> 00:08:53,027
[SPEAKER_00]: Practical is feeling freedom from it, as in freedom from craving in regard to it, which is freedom from suffering.

121
00:08:54,208 --> 00:08:58,453
[SPEAKER_00]: Because one who sees... the dhamma sees patīcā-sampāda, one who sees patīcā-sampāda sees the dhamma.

122
00:09:01,435 --> 00:09:03,436
[SPEAKER_00]: So, literally, the entire dhamma is in that principle.

123
00:09:04,016 --> 00:09:06,457
[SPEAKER_00]: And why is that principle so important, so profound?

124
00:09:06,517 --> 00:09:22,963
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, if you see that your presently enduring desire, fully embedded in the attitude of your craving for this, craving for this to not be, craving for this to be, if you see all of that, that experience in its entirety,

125
00:09:23,712 --> 00:09:46,213
[SPEAKER_00]: As fully contained, fully determined, formed, the saṅkhara, by the presently enduring feeling that you've been subjected to, you haven't created it, you realize the utmost of your desires and cravings and wills, it's actually rooted in something else, not you, and thus cannot be yours, even if you engage with it.

126
00:09:48,887 --> 00:09:52,369
[SPEAKER_00]: But by not dwelling on that long enough, you do develop the notion of I am.

127
00:09:52,890 --> 00:09:58,994
[SPEAKER_00]: My own possessions, my own feelings, my own cravings, my own desires, and everything else is the result of that.

128
00:10:00,395 --> 00:10:01,215
[SPEAKER_00]: But it's the other way around.

129
00:10:01,836 --> 00:10:04,838
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you want to start including it in a bigger context.

130
00:10:06,199 --> 00:10:12,003
[SPEAKER_00]: Context that, yeah, your mind might be feverish with the desire for this particular object.

131
00:10:13,069 --> 00:10:16,151
[SPEAKER_00]: Peripheral to that is the necessity of the attitude of craving.

132
00:10:16,231 --> 00:10:18,473
[SPEAKER_00]: Can you have desire without craving for what you desire?

133
00:10:18,793 --> 00:10:18,933
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

134
00:10:21,355 --> 00:10:24,757
[SPEAKER_00]: So you realize, okay, so whenever there is a desire, I know craving is there.

135
00:10:25,918 --> 00:10:27,579
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't need to see it written on the wall.

136
00:10:27,659 --> 00:10:28,260
[SPEAKER_00]: Craving?

137
00:10:28,580 --> 00:10:29,120
[SPEAKER_00]: No, I know.

138
00:10:29,280 --> 00:10:30,661
[SPEAKER_00]: Desire is present means craving is here.

139
00:10:31,002 --> 00:10:33,143
[SPEAKER_00]: Desire is a symptom of the attitude of craving.

140
00:10:34,604 --> 00:10:38,447
[SPEAKER_00]: Once you become familiar with that, you take it step further by the sanction.

141
00:10:39,387 --> 00:10:39,967
[SPEAKER_00]: Same principle.

142
00:10:40,488 --> 00:10:41,108
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's what we meant.

143
00:10:41,248 --> 00:10:43,350
[SPEAKER_00]: You learn how to attend properly.

144
00:10:44,131 --> 00:10:50,075
[SPEAKER_00]: So, instead of attending at what craving is pushing you towards, you attend at the necessary basis for the push of the craving.

145
00:10:51,122 --> 00:10:59,709
[SPEAKER_00]: That yoniso manasikāra, you attend to the womb, to the back, to the origin of this attitude that I have, that I'm not denying, but I'm not engaging with it either.

146
00:10:59,769 --> 00:11:08,135
[SPEAKER_00]: That's where sensory strain comes first, then you get to understand, and then you just get to be drilling and drilling the same understanding through the same way of properly attending.

147
00:11:08,175 --> 00:11:09,356
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's the noble method.

148
00:11:09,497 --> 00:11:10,898
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the noble method, exactly.

149
00:11:12,359 --> 00:11:13,820
[SPEAKER_00]: As concise as you can explain it.

